{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe MCP client URL that every doc and the committed example instruct clients to use —\n`https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp/` (WITH the trailing slash) — answers with a 307\nredirect to the **http://** scheme. The redirect target is plaintext, and no HSTS header is\npresent on the response to upgrade it back.\n\n## Evidence (unauthenticated reads; no credential was sent)\n\n    $ curl -s -o /dev/null -D - https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp/\n    HTTP/2 307\n    location: http://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp\n\n    $ curl -s -o /dev/null -D - -X POST -d '{}' https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp\n    HTTP/2 401\n    www-authenticate: Bearer error=\"invalid_token\", ...\n\nNeither response carried a `strict-transport-security` header. Re-reproduced 2026-08-27.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nA 307 PRESERVES the method and body, and clients differ in whether they retain an\n`Authorization` header across a same-host scheme change. A client that retains it transmits the\nper-client bearer PAT over plaintext. Even a client that drops the header still sends the request\nbody in the clear. Without HSTS there is no transport-layer backstop.\n\nThe exposure is widened by the documentation pointing AT the affected form:\n`examples/mcp-clients/codex/config.toml:18`, `docs/mcp-client-setup.md:54,73,96`,\n`docs/mcp-auth.md:252`.\n\n## Confirmed root cause (proven at runtime — see the RCA comment)\n\n**A — the redirect exists** because `infra/nginx/rebar.conf.template:127` is a PREFIX\n`location /mcp` with a URI-preserving `proxy_pass http://rebar_mcp;`, so the external `/mcp/`\nreaches the app verbatim while the app mounts at `http_path = \"/mcp\"`\n(`src/rebar/mcp_server.py:524,618-627`). Starlette's default `redirect_slashes=True` (set\nnowhere in this repo) emits the 307. `docs/mcp-auth.md:252-271` already prescribes EXACT-MATCH\nlocations for precisely this asymmetry; the committed template diverged from that documented\npattern.\n\n**B — the target is plaintext** because uvicorn is built at `src/rebar/_mcp_health.py:311-317`\nwith no `forwarded_allow_ips`, so it defaults from the environment\n(`uvicorn/config.py:356-357`: `os.environ.get(\"FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS\", \"127.0.0.1\")`) and wraps\nthe app in `ProxyHeadersMiddleware(trusted_hosts=...)` (`uvicorn/config.py:527`). The app runs\nin a container (`docker-compose.yml:337` binds `0.0.0.0`, published `127.0.0.1:8091:8091`), so\nnginx's connection arrives from the docker bridge gateway, not `127.0.0.1` — the peer is\nuntrusted, nginx's `X-Forwarded-Proto https` (`rebar.conf.template:132`) is dropped, and every\napp-generated absolute URL is built as `http://`. No `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` is set in `infra/`.\n\n**C — no backstop:** a repo-wide grep for `strict-transport` returns zero hits; the 443 server\nblock has no `add_header Strict-Transport-Security`.\n\nDiscriminating experiment (in-process over the real `streamable_http_app`, POST `/mcp/`,\n`follow_redirects=False`): bare app -> `307 http://…`; `trusted_hosts=\"127.0.0.1\"` + peer\n`172.17.0.1` + `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` -> `307 http://…` (reproduces production);\n`trusted_hosts=\"*\"`, same request -> `307 https://…`. The only varied factor is the trusted-peer\nsetting and it flips the scheme, killing \"the app hardcodes http\", \"nginx rewrote the Location\",\nand \"HTTP/2 stripped it\".\n\n## Approach\n\nFix at the edge, and remove the underlying scheme-derivation defect, so the guarantee does not\ndepend on any single layer:\n\n1. **Serve `/mcp/` directly instead of redirecting** — adopt the repo's own documented\n   exact-match pattern (`docs/mcp-auth.md:252-271`), specialized to the named upstream:\n   `location = /mcp/ { proxy_pass http://rebar_mcp/mcp; … }` and `location = /mcp\n   { proxy_pass http://rebar_mcp/mcp; … }`. An exact-match location with a URI on `proxy_pass`\n   replaces the matched URI, so the external `/mcp/` arrives at the app as `/mcp` and\n   `redirect_slashes` never fires. There is then NO Location header on the documented URL at\n   all. The existing PREFIX `location /mcp` is retained for `/mcp/...` sub-paths and keeps its\n   current SSE settings, so nothing that works today stops working.\n2. **Make a plaintext Location structurally impossible at the edge** — add\n   `proxy_redirect http://$host/ https://$host/;` (and the upstream-name form) to the MCP\n   locations, so nginx upgrades any `http://` Location an upstream might ever emit. This is the\n   fail-closed backstop that holds even if a future SDK change reintroduces a redirect.\n3. **Fix the scheme derivation itself** — set `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` on the `mcp` compose service\n   so uvicorn's `ProxyHeadersMiddleware` trusts nginx's forwarded proto. This needs NO code\n   change (uvicorn reads the variable natively, `uvicorn/config.py:356-357`), and it corrects\n   EVERY app-generated absolute URL, not just this one Location. Spoofing is not a new risk:\n   nginx sets `proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;` as a LITERAL, so a client-supplied\n   value is always overwritten before it reaches the app, and the container port is published on\n   host loopback only, with nginx as the sole reachable client — the posture\n   `REBAR_MCP_HTTP_TLS_AT_EDGE=true` already acknowledges.\n4. **Add HSTS** — `add_header Strict-Transport-Security \"max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains\"\n   always;` on the 443 server block, so a client cannot be walked down to plaintext even by an\n   unrelated future path. `always` is required so it is emitted on error responses (the 401 the\n   MCP endpoint returns to an unauthenticated client) too.\n\nDeliberately NOT changed: `EXPECTED_URL` in `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_client_configs.py`\nand the documented/example client URLs. After step 1 the trailing-slash form is served DIRECTLY\nover https with no redirect, so it is safe and stays canonical — keeping it also keeps\n`auth_resource_server_url` (the OAuth audience, `docs/mcp-auth.md:252`) consistent with the\ncommitted examples. Changing it would be churn that does not improve safety.\n\nAlso NOT changed: production nginx on the live box. This change proposes the config in the repo;\napplying it is an operator step (`nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx`).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `https://<box>/mcp/` no longer redirects to an `http://` target: the rendered edge config\n      routes `/mcp/` straight to the app's `/mcp` via an exact-match location, so no Location\n      header is produced for the documented URL. Oracle: structural parse of\n      `infra/nginx/rebar.conf.template` asserting an exact-match `location = /mcp/` whose\n      `proxy_pass` carries the app path.\n- [ ] An HSTS header is served on the 443 server, with `always` so it is present on the\n      unauthenticated 401 as well. Oracle: structural parse asserting\n      `add_header Strict-Transport-Security … always;` inside the 443 server block.\n- [ ] A test fails if the edge could ever emit a non-https `Location` for the MCP paths. Oracle:\n      structural parse asserting every MCP location carries a `proxy_redirect` that maps an\n      `http://` upstream Location to `https://`; the test fails when that directive is absent.\n- [ ] The documented/example client URL reaches the authenticated endpoint without a scheme\n      downgrade. Oracle: with the deployed forwarded-proto trust in force, a `/mcp/` request\n      through the real `streamable_http_app()` yields an `https` Location (contrast: an\n      untrusted peer yields `http`, the negative control that proves the assertion has teeth);\n      and `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` is asserted present on the compose `mcp` service.\n\n## Testing\n\nRED-first, both tiers observed failing before any fix:\n\n- `tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py` — structural tests over the committed nginx template\n  (exact-match `/mcp/` location, `proxy_redirect` http->https on the MCP locations, HSTS with\n  `always` in the 443 block) and over `docker-compose.yml` (`FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` on the `mcp`\n  service). No nginx binary is available in CI, so these parse the committed text — the same\n  approach the existing `test_nginx_mcp_location_proxies_to_the_named_upstream` (`:154`) uses.\n- `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_http_transport.py` — a behavioural test over the real\n  `streamable_http_app()` through `uvicorn.middleware.proxy_headers.ProxyHeadersMiddleware`,\n  asserting the Location scheme is preserved as `https` when the forwarded proto is trusted, with\n  an untrusted-peer negative control asserting `http`. This is the executable oracle for\n  mechanism B and does not depend on the nginx text.\n\nMutation check: re-introduce each confirmed cause (drop the exact-match location; drop\n`proxy_redirect`; drop the HSTS header; drop `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS`) and confirm the\ncorresponding test returns to RED, then restore. Run inside an OS sandbox.\n\n## File impact\n\n`infra/nginx/rebar.conf.template`, `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml`,\n`tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py`, `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_http_transport.py`.\n\n## Compat / rollback\n\nAdditive at the edge: two new exact-match locations ahead of the retained prefix location, two\nnew directives, one new compose env var. `/mcp` (no slash) already returns a clean 401 today and\ncontinues to; `/mcp/` stops redirecting and starts serving, which is strictly better for every\nclient (a client that followed the redirect now skips a round trip; a client that did not now\nworks). No caller depends on the 307 — it is the defect. Rollback = revert the four files.\nTrust boundary: unchanged. The `static` bearer still gates `/mcp`; `/health` stays unauthenticated;\n`FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` only affects which peer's `X-Forwarded-*` uvicorn trusts, and nginx\noverwrites that header unconditionally.\n\n## Portability\n\nThe fix is entirely in committed config + tests that parse committed text. No CI provider is\ninvolved: the structural tests run under plain `pytest` anywhere, and the behavioural test is\nin-process ASGI with no docker and no network. Applying the nginx change is an operator step on\nthe box, not a CI trigger.\n\n## Security note\n\nReported and re-verified from unauthenticated probes only; no PAT was transmitted during this\ninvestigation and no credential value appears in this ticket.\n\nCaused by: fd4a-4e91-f179-4932 (cibophobic-holohedral-esok)."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787859203445244142,"uuid":"eec5b612-d8e7-42c4-bc43-3759366a828b"}